<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
             xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
             xmlns:vm="clr-namespace:KinesisEdit.ViewModels"
             xmlns:controls="clr-namespace:KinesisEdit.Controls"
             mc:Ignorable="d" d:DesignWidth="268" d:DesignHeight="520"
             x:Class="KinesisEdit.Views.RemapPanelView"
             x:DataType="vm:RemapPanelViewModel">
  <!--
    The key inspector's Remap panel (mockups 1e/2a) — the body under the rail's mode tabs.

    IT IS NOT MODAL AND CARRIES NO OVERLAY ROLE. This is the panel that replaced the Search Keys
    overlay of §11.6: there is no scrim, no card and no `HasActiveOverlay` anywhere near it, and the
    board beside the rail stays clickable the whole time.

    TWO WAYS TO CHANGE THE ASSIGNMENT, IN THE ORDER THE USER SHOULD TRY THEM. `● Record` arms
    keystroke capture and the next physical keypress becomes the assignment — the fast path, and the
    only way this app has ever assigned from a real key. The picker below it is for the actions no
    keyboard can press: a media key, a mouse button, a profile selector.

    `● Record` SITS BESIDE THE TOKEN FIELD, NOT BESIDE THE SEARCH FIELD. Mockup 1e draws it on the
    search row; it is here instead for two reasons. The picker is reusable — the macro-insertion
    modal hosts the same control and has no keystroke capture at all — so a Record button inside it
    would be a capture dependency in a control that must not have one. And the rail is 268 px wide:
    a field, a counter and a button on one line leaves the query box too narrow to show what was
    typed into it. The pairing that results — the field, and the button that fills it — is the one
    the Tap and Hold panel uses for its own two fields.

    THE TOKEN FIELD IS A BORDER, NEVER A TEXTBOX. Focus inside a text box suspends keystroke
    capture, so a field the user could click into would swallow the very keypress it exists to
    record. `TokenField` is a Border and declares the `.armed` face this binds. The SEARCH field
    inside the picker below IS a real TextBox, and is supposed to suspend capture — a query is typed,
    not pressed.
  -->
  <Border Padding="{DynamicResource PaddingInspectorSection}">
    <StackPanel Spacing="8">

      <!-- ===== What the position sends now ===================================================
           The rail's header already says `factory d · now esc`; this is the same "now" as an
           editable surface rather than as a readout — it is what the Record button fills and what
           the picker replaces. -->
      <StackPanel Spacing="4">
        <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:RemapPanelViewModel.CurrentTokenLabel}" Classes="sectionLabel" />

        <!-- A Grid, not a horizontal StackPanel: a StackPanel measures its children with infinite
             width, so the field would never give the button room inside 244 px of rail. -->
        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" ColumnSpacing="8">
          <Border Grid.Column="0"
                  Classes="actionField"
                  Classes.armed="{Binding IsRecording}"
                  VerticalAlignment="Center">
            <TextBlock Text="{Binding CurrentToken}"
                       Classes="monoValue"
                       TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                       VerticalAlignment="Center" />
          </Border>

          <!-- Red, because handoff.md:82 gives that hue to Record as well as to Discard. The `●` is
               an Ellipse and not the mockup's U+25CF: neither embedded IBM Plex family carries the
               codepoint, so the character would tofu on a platform that substitutes nothing. -->
          <controls:AccessibleButton Grid.Column="1"
                                     Classes="recordAction"
                                     Command="{Binding RecordCommand}"
                                     VerticalAlignment="Center">
            <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="6">
              <Ellipse Classes="recordDot" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              <TextBlock Text="{Binding RecordCommandCaption}" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
            </StackPanel>
          </controls:AccessibleButton>
        </Grid>

        <!-- Said only while something is actually waiting for a key, so the panel never instructs
             a user who has not asked for anything. -->
        <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:RemapPanelViewModel.RecordingHint}"
                   Classes="body muted"
                   TextWrapping="Wrap"
                   IsVisible="{Binding IsRecording}" />
      </StackPanel>

      <!-- ===== What the app noticed ==========================================================
           Amber and non-blocking: the assignment already happened. The sentence is READ off the
           editor's advisory set, which is built from DuplicateKeyScan — nothing here rescans, and
           nothing here re-words: "Duplicates are allowed." is AdvisoryText's own closing clause. -->
      <Border Classes="pickerAdvisory" IsVisible="{Binding HasDuplicateAdvisory}">
        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*" ColumnSpacing="6">
          <Ellipse Grid.Column="0"
                   Width="6"
                   Height="6"
                   Fill="{DynamicResource StatusAdvisoryStrongBrush}"
                   VerticalAlignment="Top"
                   Margin="0,4,0,0" />
          <TextBlock Grid.Column="1"
                     Text="{Binding DuplicateAdvisory}"
                     Classes="body statusWarning"
                     TextWrapping="Wrap" />
        </Grid>
      </Border>

      <!-- ===== The refusal ===================================================================
           A locked position (05 §5.3) is the design's own exception to "absent features are not
           shown": the panel stays and explains itself rather than vanishing, because the user
           pointed at this key. The rail normally answers a locked position with its own locked-key
           panel, so this is the belt to that braces. -->
      <TextBlock Text="{Binding UnavailableReason}"
                 Classes="body statusWarning"
                 TextWrapping="Wrap"
                 IsVisible="{Binding !IsAvailable}" />

      <!-- ===== The picker ====================================================================
           Resolved by ViewLocator from the panel's own TokenPickerViewModel, like every other view
           model this app hosts as content. -->
      <ContentControl Content="{Binding Picker}" IsVisible="{Binding IsAvailable}" />
    </StackPanel>
  </Border>
</UserControl>
